- Implementation commit: `2f4daa9a475d453a2d193675f19d51c9b315d30f`
- Producer core fixture SHA-256: `1382505e8f49138746501dd77760358ae286e285f762b152f2223b912c8e6167`

## Finding and change

Pinned Java `TopicConfig.encode()` and `toString()` serialize the supplied
attributes map in its iteration order. With Java `HashMap` keys `BB` and `Aa`,
their colliding string hashes put both entries in the same bucket; Java retains
their insertion order. Rust previously stored these attributes in `BTreeMap`,
which sorted the keys and changed the public encoded string.

`TopicConfigAttributeMap` now uses `IndexMap` to retain insertion order. The
serializer applies Java hash spreading and bucket ordering while preserving
insertion order within each bucket. TopicConfig JSON and whitespace-form
decoders also retain parsed key order. Rust equality and hashing remain
independent of insertion order, matching Java Map equality/hash semantics.
The Java fixture records both insertion orders, setter and decode paths, and
the corresponding `encode()`/`toString()` results.

